I scanned 65 source/workflow scripts—about 51,000 lines—without changing anything. There are several substantial performance opportunities.
The main findings, in priority order:
The logged presence filter is not slow; matrix construction immediately afterward is.
The filter at [data_loader.py (line 1702)](/Users/nmfuphicsir.co.za/Documents/pHDProject/Code/network_parser/network_parser/data_loader.py:1702) is only a dictionary comprehension. The next loop at [data_loader.py (line 1773)](/Users/nmfuphicsir.co.za/Documents/pHDProject/Code/network_parser/network_parser/data_loader.py:1773) evaluates every sample against every retained feature:
10,974 × 31,064 = 340,898,336 Python iterations.
Each absent feature also constructs an AlleleCall object at [vcf_call_semantics.py (line 1061)](/Users/nmfuphicsir.co.za/Documents/pHDProject/Code/network_parser/network_parser/vcf_call_semantics.py:1061). With your current assume_absent_variant_is_reference=true, this could instead preallocate a zero-filled NumPy matrix and update only explicit VCF calls through a coordinate-to-column index. Alternate alleles at one position would still remain separate columns. A small parity benchmark of this approach was approximately 13.2× faster than the current cell-by-cell implementation.

Matrix construction creates several redundant, very large representations.
After the dense loop, the code simultaneously holds Python lists, allele strings, binary strings and a float64 DataFrame at [data_loader.py (line 1891)](/Users/nmfuphicsir.co.za/Documents/pHDProject/Code/network_parser/network_parser/data_loader.py:1891) and [data_loader.py (line 1914)](/Users/nmfuphicsir.co.za/Documents/pHDProject/Code/network_parser/network_parser/data_loader.py:1914). The float64 matrix alone is about 2.73 GB for this cohort.
A compact numeric matrix—uint8 plus a missingness mask, or at least float32—would reduce memory substantially. FASTA/binary strings could be generated only while writing rather than retained for every sample.

Artifact redundancy detection adds roughly another gigabyte of temporary data.
[data_loader.py (line 3066)](/Users/nmfuphicsir.co.za/Documents/pHDProject/Code/network_parser/network_parser/data_loader.py:3066) converts the entire matrix into an encoded array, transposes it, and creates a separate bytes object for every feature. At your dimensions, those representations are roughly 341 MB each.
Exact pattern grouping could use packed bits or chunked column hashes with collision verification. The configured sampling controls are currently ignored at [data_loader.py (line 1043)](/Users/nmfuphicsir.co.za/Documents/pHDProject/Code/network_parser/network_parser/data_loader.py:1043).

Chi-square filtering is implemented as 31,064 separate pandas operations—and is repeated at hierarchy nodes.
[statistical_validation_branch.py (line 274)](/Users/nmfuphicsir.co.za/Documents/pHDProject/Code/network_parser/network_parser/statistical_validation_branch.py:274) repeatedly performs index intersections, masks, pd.crosstab, SciPy calls and mutual information calculations, once per feature.
Since the matrix is binary, all contingency counts can be computed in NumPy chunks for every feature simultaneously, while preserving feature-specific missingness. Chi-square, Cramér’s V and mutual information can then be calculated from those counts. Fisher testing only needs per-feature calls for applicable 2×2 tables.
This matters particularly because filtering is invoked at each hierarchy target at [hierarchy_protocol.py (line 2218)](/Users/nmfuphicsir.co.za/Documents/pHDProject/Code/network_parser/network_parser/hierarchy_protocol.py:2218).

The validation workflow rebuilds the same VCF matrix once per label.
The experiment script launches four independent cross-validation commands at [02_Lineage_family_Lineage_AMR_profile.sh (line 83)](/Users/nmfuphicsir.co.za/Documents/pHDProject/Code/network_parser/scripts/testing_scripts/02_Lineage_family_Lineage_AMR_profile.sh:83). Every command reloads and reconstructs the same 10,974-genome cohort.
A canonical raw matrix could be built once and reused by training and all CV labels. Supervised filtering must remain fold-local to prevent leakage, but VCF parsing and raw matrix encoding do not need to be repeated.

Each trainable hierarchy node runs a large stack of model fits.
With defaults, a node performs approximately:
Four panel sizes × five folds: 20 fits
Six model probes × five folds: 30 fits
Five-fold out-of-fold threshold selection: at least 5 more fits
Optional decision-tree bootstrap: another 100–150 trees
Panel scoring occurs at [feature_panel_selection.py (line 984)](/Users/nmfuphicsir.co.za/Documents/pHDProject/Code/network_parser/network_parser/feature_panel_selection.py:984), model probing at [model_selector.py (line 278)](/Users/nmfuphicsir.co.za/Documents/pHDProject/Code/network_parser/network_parser/model_selector.py:278), and OOF threshold fitting at [ml_protocol.py (line 1648)](/Users/nmfuphicsir.co.za/Documents/pHDProject/Code/network_parser/network_parser/ml_protocol.py:1648).
Opportunities include sequentially stopping at the first passing panel when using smallest_passing, dataset-aware exclusion/downsampling of RBF SVC, staged model screening, and reusing folds and numeric preprocessing.

Hierarchy parallelism can create concurrent full-matrix copies.
Every node begins with X.loc[...].copy() at [hierarchy_protocol.py (line 2087)](/Users/nmfuphicsir.co.za/Documents/pHDProject/Code/network_parser/network_parser/hierarchy_protocol.py:2087), while child nodes may run concurrently. Worker budgeting assumes 4 GB per worker at [utils.py (line 1095)](/Users/nmfuphicsir.co.za/Documents/pHDProject/Code/network_parser/network_parser/utils.py:1095), rather than measuring the actual matrix and model requirements.
Shared immutable NumPy storage plus row indices would avoid many copies. Worker limits should be calculated from actual matrix bytes.

CSV checkpoint hashing and output cause repeated full-matrix serialization.
sha256_dataframe first turns the entire DataFrame into one in-memory CSV byte string at [utils.py (line 550)](/Users/nmfuphicsir.co.za/Documents/pHDProject/Code/network_parser/network_parser/utils.py:550). The single-label pipeline hashes the same matrices multiple times and also writes several CSV checkpoints.
Chunked numeric hashing or hashing an already-written Parquet/Arrow artifact would be considerably faster and use less peak memory.

The shared missingness layer repeatedly copies and converts numeric matrices.
[matrix_contract.py (line 160)](/Users/nmfuphicsir.co.za/Documents/pHDProject/Code/network_parser/network_parser/matrix_contract.py:160) copies the matrix, converts every column individually, and then converts the whole result again. Training preparation calls this during both fitting and transformation. Mode calculation and imputation also loop column-by-column.
Numeric matrices could bypass coercion, and binary modes/imputation could be vectorized.

Several secondary paths also have worthwhile improvements.
Recursive query inference performs pandas alignment and prediction one sample at a time; breadth-first batching by hierarchy node would be faster: [query_engine.py (line 3142)](/Users/nmfuphicsir.co.za/Documents/pHDProject/Code/network_parser/network_parser/query_engine.py:3142).
Exact FASTA mapping repeatedly scans the complete genome for every feature: [sequence_query_encoder.py (line 269)](/Users/nmfuphicsir.co.za/Documents/pHDProject/Code/network_parser/network_parser/sequence_query_encoder.py:269). Multi-pattern indexing or batched BLAST would scale better.
Sample-separation visualization uses Python O(samples² × features) loops: [hierarchy_separation_visualization.py (line 209)](/Users/nmfuphicsir.co.za/Documents/pHDProject/Code/network_parser/network_parser/hierarchy_separation_visualization.py:209).
Panel annotation scans the whole catalogue per panel row: [panel_annotation.py (line 439)](/Users/nmfuphicsir.co.za/Documents/pHDProject/Code/network_parser/network_parser/panel_annotation.py:439). Normalized joins/indexed lookups would be faster.
Decision-tree feature stability and pair stability fit separate bootstrap tree sets: [decision_tree_branch.py (line 711)](/Users/nmfuphicsir.co.za/Documents/pHDProject/Code/network_parser/network_parser/decision_tree_branch.py:711) and [decision_tree_branch.py (line 981)](/Users/nmfuphicsir.co.za/Documents/pHDProject/Code/network_parser/network_parser/decision_tree_branch.py:981). The first set of trees could retain path information for reuse.
FASTQ alignment statistics trigger three additional complete alignment scans per sample at [fastq_processor.py (line 816)](/Users/nmfuphicsir.co.za/Documents/pHDProject/Code/network_parser/network_parser/fastq_processor.py:816).

There is also a defect in the intended chi-square permutation fast path: it passes an ndarray at [statistical_validation_branch.py (line 605)](/Users/nmfuphicsir.co.za/Documents/pHDProject/Code/network_parser/network_parser/statistical_validation_branch.py:605), but the receiving function calls DataFrame methods at [statistical_validation_branch.py (line 637)](/Users/nmfuphicsir.co.za/Documents/pHDProject/Code/network_parser/network_parser/statistical_validation_branch.py:637). That path would fail before delivering its intended speedup.
The best implementation order would be: sparse coordinate-based matrix construction, vectorized chi-square testing, reusable cohort-matrix caching, reduced artifact/checkpoint duplication, then model-screening reductions. No project files or running processes were changed.